 declared at module scope, not recreated per render. Nothing to fix here — this part is already done correctly.\n\n## Caching opportunities\nTwo real gaps:\n\n1. **`countSourcesBySide()`** (`lib/server/sources.ts:23`) fetches *every column of every row* (including the `jsonb` `config` blob) and Zod-parses each one, just to count by `kind`. Push the aggregation into SQL:\n```ts\nimport { sql } from \"drizzle-orm\";\n\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  await connection();\n  const rows = await getDb()\n    .select({ kind: sources.kind, n: sql<number>`count(*)::int` })\n    .from(sources)\n    .groupBy(sources.kind);\n  const byKind = Object.fromEntries(rows.map((r) => [r.kind, r.n]));\n  return {\n    crm: (byKind.salesforce ?? 0) + (byKind.hubspot ?? 0),\n    warehouse: (byKind.databricks ?? 0) + (byKind.snowflake ?? 0),\n    synthetic: byKind.synthetic ?? 0,\n  };\n}\n```\n\n2. **No request-level memoization.** `listSources` and `countSourcesBySide` currently issue independent DB round trips. They don't collide today (different pages), but as soon as one layout needs both (e.g. a shared header showing a count plus a page showing the list), you'll double-query. Wrap in React's `cache()` so repeat calls within one render pass dedupe for free:\n```ts\nimport { cache } from \"react\";\n\nexport const listSources = cache(async (): Promise<Source[]> => {\n  await connection();\n  const rows = await getDb().select().from(sources).orderBy(sources.createdAt);\n  return rows.map((row) => sourceSchema.parse(row));\n});\n```\n\n## Memory leaks\n**None found.** No `setInterval`/`setTimeout`, no event listeners, no subscriptions, no unclosed effects anywhere in client code — there are no effects at all yet.\n\n## Redundant computations\n**None found** in app code — every mapped list is a small hardcoded const (≤6 items). The one place worth flagging isn't redundant computation so much as unindexed I/O: `matches`, `conflicts`, `resolutions`, and `audit_log` have **no indexes on their foreign key columns** (`runId`, `matchId`, `entityAId`, `entityBId`, `conflictId`). Postgres does not auto-index FKs the way it does primary keys. Given the product's own design intent — reconciling potentially large entity sets (\"Blocked to O(n·k). Never all pairs\", per the schema comments) — every future \"conflicts for this run\" or \"matches for this entity\" query will sequential-scan without these:\n```ts\nexport const matches = pgTable(\"matches\", { /* ... */ }, (table) => [\n  index(\"matches_run_idx\").on(table.runId),\n  index(\"matches_entity_a_idx\").on(table.entityAId),\n  index(\"matches_entity_b_idx\").on(table.entityBId),\n]);\n\nexport const conflicts = pgTable(\"conflicts\", { /* ... */ }, (table) => [\n  index(\"conflicts_run_idx\").on(table.runId),\n  index(\"conflicts_match_idx\").on(table.matchId),\n]);\n\nexport const resolutions = pgTable(\"resolutions\", { /* ... */ }, (table) => [\n  index(\"resolutions_conflict_idx\").on(table.conflictId),\n]);\n\nexport const auditLog = pgTable(\"audit_log\", { /* ... */ }, (table) => [\n  index(\"audit_log_run_idx\").on(table.runId),\n]);\n```\nWorth doing now via `drizzle-kit generate` while these tables are still empty, rather than as a migration against a live, populated audit log later.\n\n---\nWant me to implement any of these (the four `schema.ts` index additions, the `countSourcesBySide` rewrite, and/or the `cache()` wrap)?\n",
